307: boundary extend over the product cap by extending their invariant
308: vector fields with a collar cutoff; averaging preserves the
309: physical circle action. This carries these actual gauges in
310: (SP.13), rather than changing the cap framing.
311: 
312: \par
313: 
314: \medskip\noindent\textbf{SP.4. The relative second Chern number and the normal sphere bundle}
315: 
316: \par\nobreak
317: 
318: Cap \(W\) by the inner four-ball in its projection-normal frame
319: and the outer \(D^3\times S^1\) in its actual Cox frame. The
320: resulting base and spinor bundle will be denoted by
321: \(\overline W\) and \(\overline E\). The base orientation is
322: \[
323:  \overline W=(S^2\times D^2)\cup(D^3\times S^1)
324:                       =\partial(D^3\times D^2).
325:                                                                \tag{SP.15}
326: \]
327: The first piece has its complex orientation, and the second has
328: minus its displayed product orientation. Thus the boundary of the
329: outer cap is the negative of
330: \(K=\mathbf P^1\times S^1\), with \(K\) oriented as the
331: boundary of \(N\).
332: 
333: The bundle \(E\) extends across the inner ball and is a trivial
334: \(SU(2)\) bundle over \(N\simeq S^2\). Choose a global frame
335: agreeing there with the Cartier frame. Such agreement can be made
336: on the ball because the difference of two extending frames is
337: itself an extending gauge. Compare this frame with the Cox frame on
338: \(W\). On the inner boundary its coordinate matrix is (SP.9).
339: Since this comparison extends over \(W\), its degree on the outer
340: boundary \(K\) is the same as its degree on the inner sphere in
341: its positive complex-domain orientation.
342: 
343: We calculate that degree with its precise trace convention. The
344: first column of (SP.9) is \((y,x)\); the map
345: \((s,\rho)\mapsto(y,x)\) is complex linear with nonzero
346: determinant \(-2\). Hence its normalized boundary map has degree
347: one for the complex first-column orientation. At the identity the
348: positive tangent matrices in that orientation are
349: \[
350:  I=\begin{pmatrix}i&0\\0&-i\end{pmatrix},\quad
351:  J=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
352:  K_0=\begin{pmatrix}0&i\\i&0\end{pmatrix}.
353:                                                                \tag{SP.16}
354: \]
355: They satisfy \(IJ=-K_0\) and \(\operatorname{tr}(IJK_0)=2\).
356: Consequently the right Maurer--Cartan form has
357: \[
358:            \int_K\operatorname{tr}(dg\,g^{-1})^3=24\pi^2.
359:                                                                \tag{SP.17}
360: \]
361: This explicitly fixes the sign; the quaternion convention with the
362: opposite group orientation cannot be substituted in (SP.17).
363: 
364: Equation (SP.9) is a coordinate arrow from the frame on \(N\) to
365: the cap frame. A connection that is zero in the first frame is
366: therefore \(-dg\,g^{-1}\) in the latter frame near their boundary.
367: Writing \(\omega=dg\,g^{-1}\), we have
368: \(d\omega=\omega^2\) and
369: \[
370:  \operatorname{tr}\bigl((-\omega)d(-\omega)
371:                     +\tfrac23(-\omega)^3\bigr)
372:                     =\tfrac13\operatorname{tr}(\omega^3).
373: \]
374: Extend this connection into the cap with a collar cutoff. Stokes'
375: formula and its negative boundary orientation give the actual
376: clutch number
377: \[
378:  \begin{aligned}
379:  c_2(\overline E)[\overline W]
380:     &=\frac1{8\pi^2}\int_{\text{cap}}\operatorname{tr}(F^2)\\
381:     &=-\frac1{24\pi^2}
382:                        \int_K\operatorname{tr}(dg\,g^{-1})^3=-1.
383:  \end{aligned}                                             \tag{SP.18}
384: \]
385: For the actual adjoint bundle,
386: \(p_1(\operatorname{ad}\overline E)=-4c_2(\overline E)\).
387: This identity follows directly on the diagonal circle: the spinor
388: has Chern roots \(a,-a\), and the nontrivial real adjoint plane has
389: Euler class \(2a\). Therefore
390: \[
391:              p_1(\operatorname{ad}\overline E)[S^4]=4.    \tag{SP.19}
392: \]
393: 
394: Let \(B=S(\operatorname{ad}\overline E)\to S^4\), with the
395: actual positive Hopf meridian class \(x\) from F56. Its determinant
396: class is zero, so the oriented vertical tangent Euler class is
397: \(e_v=2x\). The exact splitting
398: \(T_vB\oplus\mathbb R\cong\pi^*(\operatorname{ad}\overline E)\)
399: gives \(4x^2=\pi^*p_1(\operatorname{ad}\overline E)\).
400: Since \(\pi_*x=1\) and \(p_1(TS^4)=0\), we obtain
401: \[
402:                          (k(B),\ell(B))=(1,4).             \tag{SP.20}
403: \]
404: Its index is zero, as already used in F56. The present calculation
405: retains the individual numbers of this actual normal bundle.
406: 
407: \par
408: 
409: \medskip\noindent\textbf{SP.5. The full oriented boundary sum}
410: 
411: \par\nobreak
412: 
413: Use exactly F56's normal-tube excision and its two product surgery
414: collars. The complex orientation of \(W\), followed by the
415: positive-Hopf normal orientation, is the ambient orientation.
416: Explicitly, F56 gives
417: \(\Omega_{\rm ambient}=-\Omega_{\rm source}\wedge d\gamma\)
418: and
419: \(\Omega_{\nu H}=-d\gamma\wedge\Omega_L\); hence
420: \(\Omega_{\rm ambient}=\Omega_W\wedge\Omega_{\nu H}\).
421: The fixed swap (SP.10) has determinant one and preserves this
422: orientation; it does not supply the initial minus. The inner
423: boundary of the removed normal disc bundle consequently has
424: orientation
425: \(-\Omega_W\wedge\Omega_{S^2}\). Both product collars preserve
426: that sign, so this additional closed boundary is \(-B\).
427: 
428: The outer boundary of \(Y\times D_\rho\) is \(-P_f\), because
429: \(\dim Y=5\). The excised inner seven-ball gives \(-N_e\).
430: Thus the complete oriented boundary, retaining all three pieces, is
431: \[
432:                          -P_f\ \sqcup\ -N_e\ \sqcup\ -B.
433:                                                                \tag{SP.21}
434: \]
435: The Thom meridian line constructed in F56 extends through this
436: cobordism and both collars. Its restriction on each displayed piece
437: is the class used above. Stokes' theorem applied to its cube and
438: to its product with the stable tangent Pontryagin class gives
439: \[
440:                   (k,\ell)(P_f)=-(k,\ell)(N_e)-(k,\ell)(B).
441:                                                                \tag{SP.22}
442: \]
443: The local raw complex-plus-height orientation reverses both the
444: six-manifold orientation and the meridian class relative to the
445: physical positive-Hopf convention. The products \(x^3\) and
446: \(p_1x\) are unchanged by these simultaneous reversals. Thus the
447: local values (SP.6) enter (SP.22) with exactly the signs written.
448: 
449: Finally the physical positive return has
450: \(P_f=M_{f^{-1}}\) in Jin's endpoint convention. Reversing the
451: parameter reverses both characteristic numbers while carrying the
452: same meridian line. Equations (SP.6), (SP.20) and (SP.22) therefore
453: give
454: \[
455:                  (k,\ell)_{\rm Jin}(f)
456:                        =(5,-4)+(1,4)=(6,0).
457:                                                                \tag{SP.23}
458: \]
459: The exact relative-coordinate identities of P3K.23 now give
460: \(m=6A-k=0\), \(n=-6A=-6\), with \(A=1\). This uses the
461: actual first-resolution product established in P3K.40. No product
462: on the other cusp curves, and no later fan value, is inferred here.
463: \end{proof}
